      ['What determines website development cost?', ['The main factors are scope, content, design complexity, CMS or application needs, integrations, SEO, accessibility, performance, testing, migration, and post-launch support. A simple brochure website and a custom portal may both be called a website, but they require very different work.', 'Cost also changes when the business needs multiple audiences, locations, languages, roles, payment flows, bookings, CRM connections, or a large content migration. Define these requirements before comparing quotes.']],
      ['Typical website project scopes', ['A focused business website usually includes discovery, content structure, responsive design, a manageable set of pages, contact forms, analytics, technical SEO foundations, testing, and deployment. A corporate website may add departments, locations, resources, approvals, integrations, and a larger content model.', 'Custom website development can include portals, dashboards, booking systems, customer accounts, workflows, APIs, and business rules. It should be estimated as a product or application rather than priced like a static marketing site.']],
      ['What a website quote should include', ['Ask whether the quote covers discovery, copy or content support, UX and visual design, development, CMS configuration, integrations, accessibility, SEO migration, analytics, testing, hosting, launch, training, and support. Missing items often reappear later as delays or extra costs.', 'Compare the proposed outcome and assumptions, not only the lowest number. A clear scope explains what is included, what the client provides, what depends on approval, and how changes are handled.']],
      ['How to plan a practical budget', ['Start with the essential user journeys and content needed for launch. Separate launch requirements from later improvements, then reserve time for content preparation, review, testing, migration, and post-launch fixes.', 'A phased release can reduce risk without cutting quality. The first phase should be dependable, accessible, measurable, and maintainable enough to produce useful evidence for the next investment.']],
      ['How Nixoware approaches website estimates', ['Nixoware reviews goals, audiences, requirements, content, integrations, constraints, timeline, and ownership before recommending a scope. The result is a practical roadmap rather than a page-count estimate that hides important work.', 'The next step is to define the business outcome and the most important customer journeys. From there, the team can separate essential scope, risks, assumptions, and future opportunities.']]
